#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:4
msgid "Understanding Device IDs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:6
msgid ""
"Every device is identified by a device ID. The device ID is used for address "
"resolution, authentication and authorization. The term \"device ID\" could "
"interchangeably have been \"key ID\" since the device ID is a direct "
"property of the public key in use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:12
msgid "Keys"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:14
msgid ""
"To understand device IDs we need to look at the underlying mechanisms. At "
"first startup, Syncthing will create a public/private keypair."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:17
msgid ""
"Currently this is a 384 bit ECDSA key (3072 bit RSA prior to v0.12.5, which "
"is what is used as an example in this article). The keys are saved in the "
"form of the private key (``key.pem``) and a self signed certificate (``cert."
"pem``). The self signing part doesn't actually add any security or "
"functionality as far as Syncthing is concerned but it enables the use of the "
"keys in a standard TLS exchange."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:24
msgid ""
"The typical certificate will look something like this, inspected with "
"``openssl x509``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:27
msgid ""
"Certificate:\n"
"    Data:\n"
"        Version: 3 (0x2)\n"
"        Serial Number: 0 (0x0)\n"
"        Signature Algorithm: sha1WithRSAEncryption\n"
"        Issuer: CN=syncthing\n"
"        Validity\n"
"            Not Before: Mar 30 21:10:52 2014 GMT\n"
"            Not After : Dec 31 23:59:59 2049 GMT\n"
"        Subject: CN=syncthing\n"
"        Subject Public Key Info:\n"
"            Public Key Algorithm: rsaEncryption\n"
"            RSA Public Key: (3072 bit)\n"
"                Modulus (3072 bit):\n"
"                    00:da:83:8a:c0:95:af:0a:42:af:43:74:65:29:f2:\n"
"                    30:e3:b9:12:d2:6b:70:93:da:0b:7b:8a:1e:e5:79:\n"
"                    ...\n"
"                    99:09:4c:a9:7b:ba:4a:6a:8b:3b:e6:e7:c7:2c:00:\n"
"                    90:aa:bc:ad:94:e7:80:95:d2:1b\n"
"                Exponent: 65537 (0x10001)\n"
"        X509v3 extensions:\n"
"            X509v3 Key Usage: critical\n"
"                Digital Signature, Key Encipherment\n"
"            X509v3 Extended Key Usage:\n"
"                TLS Web Server Authentication, TLS Web Client "
"Authentication\n"
"            X509v3 Basic Constraints: critical\n"
"                CA:FALSE\n"
"    Signature Algorithm: sha1WithRSAEncryption\n"
"        68:72:43:8b:83:61:09:68:f0:ef:f0:43:b7:30:a6:73:1e:a8:\n"
"        d9:24:6c:2d:b4:bc:c9:e8:3e:0b:1e:3c:cc:7a:b2:c8:f1:1d:\n"
"        ...\n"
"        88:7e:e2:61:aa:4c:02:e3:64:b0:da:70:3a:cd:1c:3d:86:db:\n"
"        df:54:b9:4e:be:1b"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:61
msgid ""
"We can see here that the certificate is little more than a container for the "
"public key; the serial number is zero and the Issuer and Subject are both "
"\"syncthing\" where a qualified name might otherwise be expected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:65
msgid ""
"An advanced user could replace the ``key.pem`` and ``cert.pem`` files with a "
"keypair generated directly by the ``openssl`` utility or other mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:69
msgid "Device IDs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:71
msgid ""
"To form a device ID the SHA-256 hash of the certificate data in DER form is "
"calculated. This means the hash covers all information under the "
"``Certificate:`` section above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:75
msgid ""
"The hashing results in a 256 bit hash which we encode using base32. Base32 "
"encodes five bits per character so we need 256 / 5 = 51.2 characters to "
"encode the device ID. This becomes 52 characters in practice, but 52 "
"characters of base32 would decode to 260 bits which is not a whole number of "
"bytes. The base32 encoding adds padding to 280 bits (the next multiple of "
"both 5 and 8 bits) so the resulting ID looks something like::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:82
msgid "MFZWI3DBONSGYYLTMRWGC43ENRQXGZDMMFZWI3DBONSGYYLTMRWA===="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:84
msgid ""
"The padding (``====``) is stripped away, the device ID split into four "
"groups, and `check digits <https://forum.syncthing.net/t/v0-9-0-new-node-id-"
"format/478>`__ are added for each group. For presentation purposes the "
"device ID is grouped with dashes, resulting in the final value::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:90
msgid "MFZWI3D-BONSGYC-YLTMRWG-C43ENR5-QXGZDMM-FZWI3DP-BONSGYY-LTMRWAD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:93
msgid "Connection Establishment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:95
msgid ""
"Now we know what device IDs are, here's how they are used in Syncthing. When "
"you add a device ID to the configuration, Syncthing will attempt to connect "
"to that device. The first thing we need to do is figure out the IP and port "
"to connect to. There are three possibilities here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:100
msgid ""
"The IP and port can be set statically in the configuration. The IP can "
"equally well be a host name, so if you have a static IP or a dynamic DNS "
"setup this might be a good option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:104
msgid ""
"Using local discovery, if enabled. Every Syncthing instance on a LAN "
"periodically broadcasts information about itself (device ID, address, port "
"number). If we've seen one of these broadcasts for a given device ID that's "
"where we try to connect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:109
msgid ""
"Using global discovery, if enabled. Every Syncthing instance announces "
"itself to the global discovery service (device ID and external port number - "
"the internal address is not announced to the global server). If we don't "
"have a static address and haven't seen any local announcements the global "
"discovery server will be queried for an address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:116
msgid ""
"Once we have an address and port a TCP connection is established and a TLS "
"handshake performed. As part of the handshake both devices present their "
"certificates. Once the handshake has completed and the peer certificate is "
"known, the following steps are performed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:121
msgid ""
"Calculate the remote device ID by processing the received certificate as "
"above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:123
msgid ""
"Weed out a few possible misconfigurations - i.e. if the device ID is that of "
"the local device or of a device we already have an active connection to. "
"Drop the connection in these cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:127
msgid ""
"Verify the remote device ID against the configuration. If it is not a device "
"ID we are expecting to talk to, drop the connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:130
msgid ""
"Verify the certificate ``CommonName`` against the configuration. By default, "
"we expect it to be ``syncthing``, but when using custom certificates this "
"can be changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:134
msgid "If everything checks out so far, accept the connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:137
msgid "An Aside About Collisions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:139
msgid ""
"The SHA-256 hash is cryptographically collision resistant. This means that "
"there is no way that we know of to create two different messages with the "
"same hash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:143
msgid ""
"You can argue that of course there are collisions - there's an infinite "
"amount of inputs and a finite amount of outputs - so by definition there are "
"infinitely many messages that result in the same hash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:147
msgid ""
"I'm going to quote `stack overflow <https://stackoverflow.com/"
"questions/4014090/is-it-safe-to-ignore-the-possibility-of-sha-collisions-in-"
"practice>`__ here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:151
msgid ""
"The usual answer goes thus: what is the probability that a rogue asteroid "
"crashes on Earth within the next second, obliterating civilization-as-we- "
"know-it, and killing off a few billion people ? It can be argued that any "
"unlucky event with a probability lower than that is not actually very "
"important."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:157
msgid ""
"If we have a \"perfect\" hash function with output size n, and we have p "
"messages to hash (individual message length is not important), then "
"probability of collision is about p2/2n+1 (this is an approximation which is "
"valid for \"small\" p, i.e. substantially smaller than 2n/2). For instance, "
"with SHA-256 (n=256) and one billion messages (p=10^9) then the probability "
"is about 4.3\\*10^-60."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:164
msgid ""
"A mass-murderer space rock happens about once every 30 million years on "
"average. This leads to a probability of such an event occurring in the next "
"second to about 10^-15. That's 45 orders of magnitude more probable than the "
"SHA-256 collision. Briefly stated, if you find SHA-256 collisions scary then "
"your priorities are wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:170
msgid ""
"It's also worth noting that the property of SHA-256 that we are using is not "
"simply collision resistance but resistance to a preimage attack, i.e. even "
"if you can find two messages that result in a hash collision that doesn't "
"help you attack Syncthing (or TLS in general). You need to create a message "
"that hashes to exactly the hash that my certificate already has or you won't "
"get in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:176
msgid ""
"Note also that it's not good enough to find a random blob of bits that "
"happen to have the same hash as my certificate. You need to create a valid "
"DER-encoded, signed certificate that has the same hash as mine. The "
"difficulty of this is staggeringly far beyond the already staggering "
"difficulty of finding a SHA-256 collision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:183
msgid "Problems and Vulnerabilities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:185
msgid ""
"As far as I know, these are the issues or potential issues with the above "
"mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:189
msgid "Discovery Spoofing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:191
msgid ""
"Currently, the local discovery mechanism isn't protected by crypto. This "
"means that any device can in theory announce itself for any device ID and "
"potentially receive connections for that device from the local network."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:196
msgid "Long Device IDs are Painful"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:198
msgid ""
"It's a mouthful to read over the phone, annoying to type into an SMS or even "
"into a computer. And it needs to be done twice, once for each side."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:201
msgid ""
"This isn't a vulnerability as such, but a user experience problem. There are "
"various possible solutions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:204
msgid ""
"Use shorter device IDs with verification based on the full ID (\"You entered "
"MFZWI3; I found and connected to a device with the ID MFZWI3-DBONSG-YYLTMR-"
"WGC43E-NRQXGZ-DMMFZW-I3DBON-SGYYLT-MRWA, please confirm that this is "
"correct\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/device-ids.rst:209
msgid ""
"Use shorter device IDs with an out of band authentication, a la Bluetooth "
"pairing. You enter a one time PIN into Syncthing and give that PIN plus a "
"short device ID to another user. On initial connect, both sides verify that "
"the other knows the correct PIN before accepting the connection."
msgstr ""
